{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790251625,"nonce":"NHzQtsUI08QheYUsUH5uzel7RZY=","message":"Consolidated tracker after `fcff071` (portable capabilities and format catalogs).\nThis supersedes the older “remaining” lists in earlier comments.\n\nCompleted foundation\n\n- Caribou plugin objects, closures, shared buffers, derived enums, maps,\n  nullable sequences and rooted retained descriptor values cross without a\n  serialization layer.\n- Typed `Future\u003cT\u003e` metadata crosses ABI v4, registry/describe and Haxe;\n  resource-valued futures resolve directly into core-owned plugin objects.\n- WebIDL `Promise\u003cT\u003e` return validation is implemented. Adapter/device\n  requests, buffer mapping and queue completion use shared Futures.\n- Dictionary inheritance, required/default members, typedefs, sequences,\n  records and readonly interface catalogs are imported by bindgen.\n- `GPUFeatureName` and all `GPUSupportedLimits` names are generated. Adapter\n  and device queries plus required feature/limit negotiation are exposed.\n- All 101 IDL texture formats and all 42 IDL vertex formats are generated and\n  mapped. `snorm10-10-10-2` is the sole vertex format absent from wgpu 30 and\n  fails explicitly.\n- Basic compute/readback, inferred shader layouts, textured/indexed drawing,\n  surfaces and presentation work through the `gpu` namespace.\n\nPriority 1: explicit resource layouts and binding\n\n- Generate and implement bind-group-layout entry descriptors for uniform,\n  storage and read-only-storage buffers; filtering/non-filtering/comparison\n  samplers; sampled, multisampled, depth and storage textures; and external\n  textures.\n- Add `GpuBindGroupLayout` and `GpuPipelineLayout` resources and device\n  creation methods.\n- Let bind-group entries state their binding number instead of relying on\n  insertion order.\n- Add buffer offset/size ranges, minimum binding sizes and dynamic-offset\n  flags.\n- Pass dynamic offsets to compute and render `setBindGroup`, and support all\n  bind-group indices in compute passes.\n- Allow compute and render pipelines to use either an explicit pipeline\n  layout or automatic layout inference.\n- Add a fixture with separate uniform/storage groups, a buffer subrange and a\n  dynamic uniform offset.\n\nPriority 2: complete portable pipeline, pass and transfer descriptors\n\n- Replace the render-pipeline builder projection with generated WebGPU\n  descriptors: multiple vertex buffers/attributes, programmable constants,\n  distinct vertex/fragment modules and entry points, targets/blends,\n  multisampling, primitive state and full depth/stencil state.\n- Add async compute/render pipeline creation with typed Futures.\n- Complete render-pass attachments: load/store operations, resolve targets,\n  depth/stencil read-only flags, timestamp writes, occlusion query sets and\n  maximum draw count.\n- Complete draw arguments: first vertex/index/instance, base vertex and full\n  indexed/indirect variants.\n- Complete copy descriptors: texture origin, mip level, aspect, rows per\n  image, arbitrary extents and buffer layout offsets.\n- Support write-texture layout fields and writable mapped-buffer ranges, not\n  only the current readback projection.\n\nPriority 3: errors, loss and introspection\n\n- Implement push/pop error scopes as typed Futures and expose structured\n  validation/out-of-memory/internal errors.\n- Expose `device.lost`, uncaptured errors and destruction reasons without the\n  current string queue projection.\n- Return structured shader compilation messages and source locations.\n- Add labels and standard resource metadata/getters where the WebGPU API\n  exposes them.\n- Expose adapter info fields, fallback status and subgroup sizes as structured\n  data rather than only name/driver strings.\n\nPriority 4: remaining portable WebGPU resources\n\n- Query sets, timestamp resolution and occlusion queries.\n- Render bundle encoders and bundles.\n- External textures and image/canvas/video copy sources.\n- Full surface capability/configuration API: usage, present mode, alpha mode,\n  view formats, color space and desired frame latency.\n- Texture/view descriptor details still missing from wgpu 30, notably\n  component swizzle and texture binding-view dimension, must remain explicit\n  capability/version gaps.\n- Complete the three-element sequence spelling of `GPUExtent3D` alongside\n  the existing typed dictionary spelling.\n\nRemaining bindgen/IDL work\n\n- Represent arbitrary multi-type WebIDL unions with generated tagged\n  Caribou values; current support only handles nullable/undefined cases or\n  explicit declaration overrides.\n- Generate callback/function types and callback interfaces.\n- Generate interface operations, attributes, overloads, optional/default\n  arguments, setlike/iterable members and mixins. At present resource traits\n  are explicit and `#[idl(\"Interface.operation\")]` validates the return type.\n- Validate and lower operation parameters from IDL, not only returns.\n- Add reusable generated conversion hooks from descriptor records into wgpu\n  descriptors; conversions are currently handwritten in `backend.rs`.\n- Cover remaining WebIDL object carriers such as ArrayBuffer views, image\n  sources and browser canvas/video types without introducing copies where the\n  host can lend storage.\n- Preserve unsupported extended attributes as actionable validation rules\n  where relevant (`EnforceRange`, `Clamp`, exposure/secure-context markers).\n\nwgpu-native extensions beyond the WebGPU IDL\n\n- Native feature and texture-format capability queries.\n- Binding arrays, non-uniform indexing and partially bound arrays.\n- Pipeline statistics, encoder/pass timestamps and unrestricted mapped\n  buffers.\n- Multi-draw/count, push/immediate data, texture atomics, 64-bit shader and\n  vertex capabilities, subgroup controls and native shader extensions.\n- Mesh/task shaders, acceleration structures and ray tracing behind adapter\n  feature checks.\n- Backend-specific instance/device options, memory hints, pipeline caches and\n  tracing where they can be represented portably in Caribou.\n\nTarget/runtime integration and validation\n\n- Caribou browser/wasm runtime must statically register plugins and provide\n  HTML/offscreen canvas handles. The plugin already compiles for\n  `wasm32-unknown-unknown` and schedules browser requests with `spawn_local`.\n- Validate Emscripten GLES, Android Vulkan/GLES, Windows DX12, desktop Vulkan\n  and browser WebGPU at runtime; a successful target compile is not runtime\n  conformance.\n- Add mobile/browser window-handle providers; the current window plugin\n  supplies desktop handles.\n- Decide the resource-table/thread model for future threaded wasm rather than\n  relying on the single-thread fragile Send/Sync configuration.\n- Add backend conformance fixtures for each completed group, cancellation and\n  early Future settlement, resource destruction/leak behavior, device loss,\n  validation errors and real presentation. The current host has no usable\n  Metal adapter, so local tests cover generation/catalog behavior and reach\n  the expected asynchronous adapter rejection.\n\nSuggested implementation order is the priority order above. Explicit layouts\ncome next because they unlock uniform arenas, multiple resource groups and\nreusable compatible pipelines; the later pipeline/pass APIs depend on them.","files":null}]}